  "experimental_setup": {
    "evaluation_set": "Mutual correctly classified holdout spam emails across all 5 models",
    "n_emails_per_model": 87,
    "random_baseline_repeats": 20,
    "bootstrap_ci_level": 0.95,
    "bootstrap_iterations": 1000,
    "token_universe_agreement": "active unigrams present in the email recognized by both methods"
  },
  "methodological_hypotheses": {
    "linear_models_circularity": "For linear models (Logistic Regression, Linear SVM), deletion test is partly circular because exact token weights w_j * x_j are closed-form components of the logit. The most informative evaluations are therefore LIME vs exact and Random Forest vs the linear models.",
    "naive_bayes_lime_divergence": "Naive Bayes is strictly additive in log-space (log-odds = b + sum x_j w_j). The lower rank correlation observed between exact NB log-odds and LIME is hypothesized to stem from two factors: (1) severe probability saturation/overconfidence in Naive Bayes (predict_proba outputs often cluster within 1e-6 of 0 or 1), creating a nearly flat or step-like probability surface during LIME random token masking; and (2) objective discrepancy: LIME fits an L2-regularized linear surrogate predicting probability shifts, whereas exact attribution decomposes log-odds."
  },
